   "name": "Pie Charts",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Pie charts"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a pie chart?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A** pie chart** is a circle divided into sectors which is used to **present data**\n- The **sectors** represent different **categories**\n\n  - They show the relative **proportions** of the categories\n  - They do** not** show the actual **frequencies **of each category"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I draw a pie chart?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This is easiest explained through an example\n- The following data shows the favourite colours of a class of students"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "md": "| Colour | Red | Purple | Blue | Green | Yellow | Orange |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Students | 11 | 4 | 9 | 3 | 2 | 1 |"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Write the frequencies as **fractions** of the **total number** of students, 30"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "md": "| Colour | Red | Purple | Blue | Green | Yellow | Orange |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Students | 11 | 4 | 9 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| Fractions | $\\frac{11}{30}$ | $\\frac{4}{30}$ | $\\frac{9}{30}$ | $\\frac{3}{30}$ | $\\frac{2}{30}$ | $\\frac{1}{30}$ |"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Find the **angles** of the sectors by **multiplying each fraction **by** 360°**\n\n  - Then check all angles add up to 360°"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "md": "| Colour | Red | Purple | Blue | Green | Yellow | Orange |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Students | 11 | 4 | 9 | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n| Fractions | $\\frac{11}{30}$ | $\\frac{4}{30}$ | $\\frac{9}{30}$ | $\\frac{3}{30}$ | $\\frac{2}{30}$ | $\\frac{1}{30}$ |\n| Angles | 132° | 48° | 108° | 36° | 24° | 12° |"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Draw a vertical line from the circle's centre to the top, then use a **protractor** to mark off the first angle\n\n  - Draw a line from the centre to this first mark\n  \n    - Then, from this line, mark off the next angle (and so on)"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/01/61754-pie-charts-3.png",
   "file": "assets/71161e0a8d6e-61754-pie-charts-3.png",
   "alt": "A pie chart showing the favourite colours of students",
   "caption": "Example of a pie chart"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I solve problems with pie charts?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Use the following facts\n\n  - **angles** are **proportional **to the **frequencies** of each category\n  - **360° **represents the** total frequency **\n- For harder problems, it helps to work out\n\n  - **what frequency** is represented **by 1°**\n  - **what angle** is represented by** 1 unit **of **frequency**\n- For example, if a sector of 30° represents 15 people, then\n\n  - 1° = 0.5 people (dividing by 30)\n  - 2° = 1 person (multiplying by 2)\n- These relationships can then be scaled** up** or **down** accordingly\n\n  - If 1° = 0.5 people\n  \n    - then 360° = 180 people (multiplying by 360)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "If the pie chart says 'not to scale', then examiners want you to use ratio and proportion methods to answer the questions. Don't measure angles using a protractor!"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The following pie chart represents the values of items stocked in a sports shop.\n\nThe diagram is not to scale.\n\n![A pie chart showing the value of items in a sports shop](assets/384c3ff3bcff-19750-pie-charts-4.png)\n\n(a) Given that the shop stocks £12 000 of golf items, find the total value of the shop’s stock.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n> *Find a relationship between an angle and a value*\n\n*90° = £12 000*\n\n> *The total value is represented by 360°*\n*Multiply by 4 to get from 90° to 360°*\n\n*360° = 4 ×12 000*\n\n**Final answer:** **Total value is £48 000**\n\n> *You can also do this question by finding 1° first *\n\n(b) If the angle on the pie chart for tennis is 72°, find the value of tennis items that are stocked by the shop.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n> *It is quickest to find the fraction *$\\frac{72}{360}$* of the total value, found in part (a)*\n\n$\\frac{72}{360}\\times48000=9600$\n\n**Final answer:** **The value of tennis items is £9 600**"
  }
 ]
}